  "worked_solution": "Remember that ${Q}_{0}^{+}$ is the set of all positive rational numbers and $0.$\n\n- $0$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\u2713$\n\n- $\\sqrt{27}=3\\sqrt{3}$ is not a rational number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $-\\sqrt{4}=-2$ is a negative number, so it does *not* belong to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\\times $\n\n- $\\frac{1}{9}$ is a rational number because it is the ratio of two integers, and moreover, it is a positive rational number. Therefore, $\\frac{1}{9}$ belongs to ${Q}_{0}^{+}.\\,\u2713$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and IV only\".",
